            <p class="text-lg" data-lang-key="gtIntroText"><strong>A lawyer confirms that you can own the apartment.</strong> A civil engineer confirms that it is legally permitted. Neither of them tells you what the building is going to cost you over the next five years. That is the single question this audit exists to answer.</p>

            <h2 data-lang-key="gtArrivesTitle">What arrives</h2>

            <p data-lang-key="gtArrivesIntroText">A written technical report on the observed condition of the asset. The specimen report runs to <strong>16 pages</strong> and contains:</p>

            <ul data-lang-key="gtArrivesListText">
                <li>A flagged items summary at the front, so the findings that need a decision are on page four rather than page fourteen</li>
                <li>Every inspection point scored on a fixed four-tier scale</li>
                <li>Photographic evidence attached to each finding, timestamped on the day of the visit</li>
                <li>Thermal imagery where a temperature anomaly is present</li>
                <li>A <strong>5-year CapEx</strong> forecast for the asset</li>
                <li>The full scope limitation notice, so you know exactly what the report does and does not cover</li>
            </ul>

            <p data-lang-key="gtArrivesClosingText">The report is issued in English. It is yours, and it travels with the asset.</p>

            <h2 data-lang-key="gtClassesTitle">The six asset classes</h2>

            <p data-lang-key="gtClassesIntroText">Every audit covers the same six asset classes, in the same order, on every property. A report from January therefore means the same thing as a report from June. In total, that is <strong>{{ audit.scoredPoints }}</strong> scored inspection points across five technical lots, plus an <strong>{{ audit.surveyPoints }}</strong>-point general environment survey.</p>

